window.docContents[150].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-injectivity":{"contents":"The homomorphism into the automorphism group is injective: if permuting coordinates\nby σ and then translating by t fixes every vertex, then evaluating at 0 shows\nt = 0, and evaluating at the basis vectors shows σ = 1. First a small helper: the\nmap i ↦ e i is injective.\n\nlemma single_true_left_inj {n : ℕ} {i j : Fin n}\n    (h : (e i : Fin n → Bool) = e j) : i = j := by\n  simpa [Pi.single_apply] using congrFun h i\n\n\nlemma hyperoctahedralToAut_injective (n : ℕ) :\n    Function.Injective (hyperoctahedralToAut n) := by\n  rw [injective_iff_map_eq_one]\n  rintro ⟨t, σ⟩ h\n  have happ (x : Fin n → Bool) : t.toAdd + x ∘ σ.symm = x := DFunLike.congr_fun h x\n  obtain rfl : t = 1 := by simpa using happ 0\n  obtain rfl : σ = 1 := Equiv.ext fun j => Eq.symm <| by\n    simpa [Pi.single_apply, Equiv.symm_apply_eq] using\n      congrFun (happ (e j)) j\n  rfl\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.3. Injectivity","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-injectivity"},"/Theorems/#section-the-szemer-di-regularity-lemma":{"contents":"The regularity lemma says that every graph can be approximated by a structured\none: its vertices can be partitioned into a bounded number of almost equal parts so\nthat most pairs of parts are ε-uniform.\n\nAn \\varepsilon-uniform partition; the band between two parts is shaded by their edge density\n\nIn Mathlib's statement, P.IsEquipartition says the parts of P differ in size by\nat most 1, and P.IsUniform G ε says that at most an ε fraction of the pairs of\nparts are not ε-uniform. The number of parts is at least a chosen l and at most\nbound ε l, which crucially depends only on ε and l, not on the graph.\n\n#check szemeredi_regularity\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Regularity and Removal*","header":"13.4.2. The Szemerédi Regularity Lemma","id":"/Theorems/#section-the-szemer-di-regularity-lemma"},"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-paths-and-cycles":{"contents":"A Hamiltonian path is a walk that visits every vertex of the graph exactly once.\n\n#source Walk.IsHamiltonian\n\n\nNotice that IsHamiltonian is defined using List.count, and counting the\noccurrences of a vertex in a list needs to decide whether two vertices are equal.\nThis is why [DecidableEq V] appears in almost every statement about Hamiltonian\nwalks below.\n\nA Hamiltonian walk is a path. (Notice that this is a theorem, not part of the\ndefinition.)\n\n#check Walk.IsHamiltonian.isPath\n\n\nA path is Hamiltonian iff it contains every vertex of the graph.\n\n#check Walk.IsPath.isHamiltonian_iff\n\n\nThe length of a Hamiltonian path is exactly one less than the number of vertices in\nthe graph.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (p : G.Walk u v) (hp : p.IsHamiltonian) :\n    p.length = Fintype.card V - 1 := hp.length_eq\n\n\nA walk is a finite object: its support is a list. So in the same way that an\nEulerian trail in a graph provides a Fintype instance for its edge set, a\nHamiltonian path provides a Fintype instance for the vertex set (provided we also\nhave DecidableEq V).\n\n#source Walk.IsHamiltonian.fintype\n\n\nA Hamiltonian cycle is a cycle containing every vertex of the graph. In Mathlib\nthis is defined as a cycle whose tail is a Hamiltonian path.\n\n#source Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle -- structure IsHamiltonianCycle (p : G.Walk a a) :\n                                --      Prop extends p.IsCycle where\n                                --   isHamiltonian_tail : p.tail.IsHamiltonian\n\n\nThe figure below shows a Hamiltonian path and a Hamiltonian cycle on the same\nfive-vertex graph. Both visit every vertex, but, just as for any path or cycle, they\nneed not use every edge: the grey chords are left untraversed (contrast this with an\nEulerian trail, which must use every edge).\n\nA Hamiltonian path and a Hamiltonian cycle on the same graph\n\nA rotation of a walk is a Hamiltonian cycle iff the walk is a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\n#check Walk.isHamiltonianCycle_rotate\n\n\nMathlib also has the SimpleGraph.IsHamiltonian predicate, which is a property of\nfinite graphs rather than walks. A graph on a finite vertex set is Hamiltonian if\nit contains a Hamiltonian cycle or is a single vertex.\n\n#source SimpleGraph.IsHamiltonian -- def IsHamiltonian (G : SimpleGraph α) : Prop :=\n                                  --   Fintype.card α ≠ 1 →\n                                  --     ∃ a, ∃ p : G.Walk a a, p.IsHamiltonianCycle\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Trails, Paths, Circuits and Cycles","header":"8.4.6. Hamiltonian Paths and Cycles","id":"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-paths-and-cycles"},"/Walks/#section-reachability":{"contents":"As we noted at the start of the chapter, G.Walk u v is a type rather than a\nproposition. The proposition that there is some walk from u to v is\nG.Reachable u v.\n\n#source Reachable -- def Reachable (u v : V) : Prop := Nonempty (G.Walk u v)\n\n\nReachability is an equivalence relation.\n\nexample : G.Reachable u u := Reachable.refl u\nexample (h : G.Reachable u v) : G.Reachable v u := h.symm\nexample (h : G.Reachable u v) (h' : G.Reachable v w) : G.Reachable u w := h.trans h'\n\n\nAdjacent vertices are reachable from each other.\n\nexample (h : G.Adj u v) : G.Reachable u v := h.reachable\n\n\nSince Walk.bypass turns any walk into a path, there is a path between any two\nreachable vertices.\n\n#check Reachable.exists_isPath\n\n\nWe study reachability and connectivity in detail in Connectivity.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Trails, Paths, Circuits and Cycles","header":"8.4.2. Reachability","id":"/Walks/#section-reachability"}});